  "findings": {
    "chronology_and_robustness": "Only discovery ran. Walk-forward, stress, delay, immediate-neighbor, chronological-stability, parameter-stability, and regime-stability evidence does not exist and must not be inferred.",
    "concentration": "Every candidate failed the participation-bucket concentration gate. Eight of nine failed or had undefined session concentration, and seven of nine failed or had undefined symbol concentration.",
    "cost_sensitivity": "Normal and zero-cost decisions matched. Four candidates were positive under both scenarios, two were negative under both, and three were inactive. Friction reduced positive returns but did not create the rejection; sparse and concentrated evidence dominated.",
    "gross_signal": "The 1.2-floor candidates at horizons 8, 16, and 24 produced positive Normal returns. The 16-bar candidate had the strongest observed discovery return and gross edge, but no row passed the frozen activity and concentration gates.",
    "holding_time_and_turnover": "Every active session held exactly 24 bars. Turnover followed activation count; the campaign did not reach any delay comparison.",
    "sample_size": "Each parent had 87 discovery sessions, of which 10 were causal lookback-ineligible and 77 signal-eligible. Active counts ranged from zero to six against the frozen minimum of 12; round trips ranged from zero to 12 against 24.",
    "transaction_costs": "Positive rows had cost-to-gross-profit ratios from 0.0229916953519193135359298971 to 0.06402127424884934226578441934. The sparse negative 24-bar/1.5 row had a ratio of 0.5014971929818185994178816817. Costs were not the common rejection mechanism.",
